## Overview

The **Cosmological Simulation Engine (CSE)** is a production-scale, high-performance astrophysics workstation designed for multi-node GPU clusters. The architecture is engineered from the ground up to solve the modern "crisis in cosmology" by demonstrating that a stable cosmic web can be dynamically simulated using **ordinary baryonic matter (5%) alone**, completely removing the requirement for unobserved placeholders like dark matter or dark energy.

To achieve this without breaking physical conservation laws, the engine implements a fully conservative, symplectic, adaptive-softening **$\nabla\epsilon$ corrective framework**. When particle-mesh operations scale dynamically with local phase-space density, the spatial gradient of the softening parameter ($\nabla\epsilon$) is evaluated analytically in real-time, eliminating the non-physical numerical heating that typically causes dark-matter-free models to collapse or fly apart.

